---
import "@void-drift/core/styles/buttons.css";
import "@void-drift/core/styles/surfaces.css";
import ControllerGeometryBench from "../../../components/studies/in-game-controllers/ControllerGeometryBench.svelte";
import CssGutterBench from "../../../components/studies/in-game-controllers/CssGutterBench.svelte";
import FlapStackBench from "../../../components/studies/in-game-controllers/FlapStackBench.svelte";
import GlassPaneBench from "../../../components/studies/in-game-controllers/GlassPaneBench.svelte";
import PadUxBench from "../../../components/studies/in-game-controllers/PadUxBench.svelte";
import StageMarginBench from "../../../components/studies/in-game-controllers/StageMarginBench.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((item) => item.slug === "in-game-controllers");
if (!study) throw new Error("Missing study metadata for in-game-controllers");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section aria-labelledby="part-01">
    <h2 id="part-01">
      01 / Side-margin 60° thrust flaps across thumbs and keyboards
    </h2>
    <p>
      On a keyboard, a pilot flies with three direct commands: left engine (<code
        >A</code
      >
      / <code>←</code>), right engine (<code>D</code>
      / <code>→</code>), and a single-key forward burn (<code>W</code>
      / <code>↑</code>) that fires both thrusters together. To give touch
      players the same single-thumb access to both turning and full forward
      thrust, the controller layout establishes four fixed givens from the
      concept draft:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Ship-free side margins:</strong>
        Both control pairs sit flush against the left and right screen edges,
        inside the outer horizontal gutter where camera framing and padding keep
        the ship from traveling.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lower-corner full thrust, upper-margin turn:</strong>
        Full forward thrust (<code>F</code>, firing both engines) anchors both
        lower corners; single-engine turn (<code>L</code>
        on the left,
        <code>R</code>
        on the right) sits directly above <code>F</code> along the lower-middle
        side edge.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stacked 60° angled flaps:</strong>
        Each side uses two overlapping 60°-angled flaps (sloping 30° downward
        from horizontal inward from the screen bezel), with the lower
        <code>F</code>
        flap tucked over the base of the upper <code>L</code> /
        <code>R</code>
        flap.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      With the locations and 60°-angled flap silhouette established, this study
      investigates how the side and vertical gutters are sized in CSS pixels so
      they remain a sensible minimum on every screen without bloating on larger
      displays, how the central fly area adapts across screen sizes, and how the
      stacked flaps layer and style their states.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="part-02">
    <h2 id="part-02">02 / Exploration</h2>

    <details class="prior-iteration" id="part-02-1">
      <summary>
        02.1 / Initial raw viewport sizing (superseded by stage &amp; gutter
        models)
      </summary>
      <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          <strong>Observation from 02.1:</strong>
          Plotting the 60° flaps across unconstrained device viewports (<code
            >19.5:9</code
          >
          phone through
          <code>4:3</code>
          tablet and desktop) revealed that the software had not yet separated
          the
          <em>rendered space area</em>
          from the
          <em>active camera framing box</em>
          and the
          <em>side instrumentation gutters</em>.
        </p>
        <ControllerGeometryBench client:load />
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="prior-iteration" id="part-02-2">
      <summary>
        02.2 / Stage envelope (16:9 to 4:3) with stage-scaled logical margins
      </summary>
      <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          Step 02.2 constrained the stage between <strong>16:9</strong>
          (<code>1920 × 1080</code>
          logical) and
          <strong>4:3</strong>
          (<code>1440 × 1080</code>
          logical) and tested a side margin defined in
          <em>logical stage units</em>
          (<code>H / 6 = 180</code>
          logical units at
          <code>1080</code>
          height). Because logical units scale with stage height, that margin
          shrinks to <code>128 CSS px</code> on an iPad (<code>1024 × 768</code
          >) and <code>62 CSS px</code> on a <code>375 CSS px</code> phone,
          while scaling up on tall desktop monitors.
        </p>

        <StageMarginBench client:load />
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="prior-iteration" id="part-02-3">
      <summary>
        02.3 / 180 CSS-pixel side gutters, minimum bounds, and adaptive fly
        width (accepted: 180 CSS px)
      </summary>
      <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          <strong>Decision from 02.3:</strong>
          Both left and right instrumentation and control gutters use a fixed
          <strong><code>180 CSS px</code></strong>
          width (<code>G_x = 180 CSS px</code>), leaving a sensible minimum
          margin on every screen without bloating on larger displays and
          allowing the central fly area (<code>W − 360 CSS px</code>) to expand
          naturally on wider viewports.
        </p>

        <CssGutterBench client:load />
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="prior-iteration" id="part-02-4">
      <summary>
        02.4 / Nested visual <code>F</code> tab inside outer <code>L / R</code>
        tab vs. end-to-end touch areas (settled: corner-nested 84% W, 54% H)
      </summary>
      <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          <strong
            >Settled in 02.4: Corner-nested <code>F</code> tab at 84% outer
            width (<code>151 CSS px</code>
            inside the <code>180 CSS px</code>
            outer <code>L / R</code> tab, leaving a <code>29 CSS px</code> outer
            rim) and 54% of the total tab height, paired with full-width
            end-to-end touch areas split along the 30° seam.</strong
          >
        </p>

        <FlapStackBench client:load />
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="prior-iteration" id="part-02-5" open>
      <summary>
        02.5 / Pad resting fill, border outline, markings, and active-state UX
        (findings: borderless blue glass + technical arrow-and-text markings)
      </summary>
      <div class="details-body">
        <p>
          <strong>Findings from 02.5:</strong>
          The bluish glass panes with the technical arrow-and-text markings (<code
            >◀ TURN · LEFT ENGINE</code
          >
          /
          <code>▲ BURN · BOTH ENGINES</code>) read best, but the resting blue
          glass should be roughly half as dense (much more translucent) with
          <strong>no border lines anywhere</strong>. When active, only the
          engaged pad(s) should shift from translucent blue glass to
          almost-identical-translucency yellow-brand glass: turning left/right
          tints only the upper <code>L / R</code> pane; burning forward tints
          both lower <code>F</code> panes; and burning forward + turning left
          tints both <code>F</code> and <code>L</code>.
        </p>

        <PadUxBench client:load />
      </div>
    </details>

    <h3 id="part-02-6">
      02.6 / Borderless disjoint blue-to-yellow glass panes, stepped lower-pane
      density, and aligned white technical markings
    </h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Accepted in 02.6:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Disjoint non-overlapping glass panes:</strong>
        Painting the full outer tab yellow and stacking a translucent blue
        <code>F</code>
        pane on top mixes the two tints. Instead, the upper
        <code>L / R</code>
        pane and the corner-nested <code>F</code> pane (<code
          >84% W = 151 CSS px</code
        >, <code>54% H</code>) are drawn as
        <strong>disjoint non-overlapping polygons</strong>
        with zero border lines.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stepped density with a very thin active yellow wash:</strong>
        Because the panes are borderless and non-overlapping, the lower corner
        <code>F</code>
        pane uses a slightly denser glass tint than the upper
        <code>L / R</code>
        pane both at rest (<code>36%</code>
        vs. <code>22%</code>
        <code>--color-elev-3</code>
        blue glass) and when active, where both shift to a very thin yellow
        brand wash (<code>9%</code>
        upper
        <code>L / R</code>
        and <code>16%</code> lower <code>F</code>
        <code>--color-selected</code>) so stars remain clearly visible through
        the active panes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >White technical markings aligned to the same screen-border
          distance:</strong
        >
        The two-line arrow + caption markings (<code>◀ TURN · LEFT ENGINE</code>
        and <code>▲ BURN · BOTH ENGINES</code>) use white (<code
          >--color-text</code
        >, keeping Android green reserved for the live holographic HUD), share
        the exact same horizontal distance from the outer screen border (<code
          >72 CSS px</code
        >), and place the upper <code>TURN</code> label along the slanted
        centerline of the upper corridor.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>What to notice &amp; limitations:</strong>
      The bench opens on the accepted configuration (disjoint panes,
      <code>22%</code>
      / <code>36%</code> resting blue glass,
      <code>9%</code>
      / <code>16%</code> thin active yellow wash, and
      <code>72 CSS px</code>
      border-aligned white labels).
    </p>

    <GlassPaneBench client:load />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="part-03">
    <h2 id="part-03">
      03 / Readiness: CSS-pixel gutters, multi-pointer input, and disjoint
      glass-pane wiring
    </h2>
    <p>
      Inspecting the current implementation against the accepted
      <code>180 CSS px</code>
      side gutters, corner-nested disjoint 60° glass panes (<code
        >84% W = 151 CSS px</code
      >, <code>54% H</code>), and end-to-end touch areas shows three clean
      adaptations with no architectural blockers:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Screen and gutter geometry (<code
            >apps/web/src/lib/game-screen.ts</code
          >):</strong
        >
        <code>fitGameScreen(width, height)</code>
        currently computes a stage-scaled logical gutter (<code
          >(1080 / 6) * scale</code
        >). Adopting a fixed <code>180 CSS px</code> side gutter (<code
          >G_x = 180</code
        >) and a minimum vertical gutter (<code>G_y = 48 CSS px</code>, clamped
        on short phones) lets the rendered space canvas fill the safe viewport
        while exposing the central active camera-fitting box (<code
          >width - 360 CSS px</code
        >
        by <code>height - 2·G_y CSS px</code>) independently of the outer stage
        width.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Independent pad states &amp; dual-thrust input (<code
            >packages/core/src/lib/entities/Input.ts</code
          >):</strong
        >
        Extending <code>setPointer(id, zone)</code> to accept
        <code>"left" | "right" | "both" | null</code>
        lets the input manager aggregate <code>"both"</code> into
        <code>leftThruster: true</code>
        and
        <code>rightThruster: true</code>
        (matching keyboard <code>W</code> /
        <code>ArrowUp</code>) while exposing independent active flags for the
        upper turn panes (<code>L</code>, <code>R</code>) and lower burn panes (<code
          >F</code
        >) so turning lights only <code>L</code> or
        <code>R</code>, forward burn lights only the lower <code>F</code> panes,
        and forward + turn lights both.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >Disjoint visual glass panes vs. end-to-end pointer capture in
          <code>FlightDeck.svelte</code>:</strong
        >
        Replacing the rectangular <code>148 × 64 px</code>
        <code>.engine.cut-surface</code>
        buttons in
        <code>FlightDeck.svelte</code>
        separates each side's
        <code>180 CSS px</code>
        gutter assembly into:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Disjoint borderless SVG glass panes:</strong>
            Draws the L-shaped upper <code>L / R</code> polygon (<code
              >22%</code
            >
            resting <code>--color-elev-3</code> blue glass →
            <code>9%</code>
            active <code>--color-selected</code> yellow wash) and the
            corner-nested lower <code>F</code> polygon (<code>151 CSS px</code>
            wide, <code>54%</code> height;
            <code>36%</code>
            resting blue glass → <code>16%</code> active yellow wash), with
            white (<code>--color-text</code>) technical labels (<code
              >◀ TURN · LEFT ENGINE</code
            >
            /
            <code>▲ BURN · BOTH ENGINES</code>) aligned at
            <code>72 CSS px</code>
            from the outer screen border.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>End-to-end pointer capture layer:</strong>
            Classifies pointer events across the full <code>180 CSS px</code>
            gutter width against the single 30° seam line, supporting live thumb
            slides between <code>L / R</code> and <code>F</code> and clearing on
            <code>pointerup</code>, <code>pointercancel</code>,
            <code>lostpointercapture</code>, <code>blur</code>, pause, or
            portrait rotation.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="part-04">
    <h2 id="part-04">
      04 / Scope: three slices to 180 CSS px gutters and disjoint 60° glass
      thrust pads
    </h2>
    <p>
      <strong>No <code>docs/design.md</code> change.</strong>
      The controller pads and <code>180 CSS px</code> side gutters use existing
      design tokens (<code>--color-elev-3</code>,
      <code>--color-selected</code>, <code>--color-text</code>, and
      <code>--font-family-ui</code>); their contracts belong in
      <code>docs/specs/game/game-screen/spec.md</code>
      and
      <code>docs/specs/game/controls/spec.md</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>
      S1 — CSS-pixel gutter geometry in <code>game-screen</code> (web, small)
    </h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Update <code>apps/web/src/lib/game-screen.ts</code> and
        <code>apps/web/src/components/FlightDeck.svelte</code>
        so side instrumentation gutters use a fixed
        <code>180 CSS px</code>
        width (<code>G_x = 180</code>) and sensible top/bottom minimum bounds,
        leaving the central active camera-framing width as
        <code>width - 360 CSS px</code>
        while space renders edge-to-edge behind the gutters.
      </li>
      <li>
        Update <code>docs/specs/game/game-screen/spec.md</code> to record the
        <code>180 CSS px</code>
        side gutters and adaptive central fly width.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Correctness:</strong>
        Unit and browser checks verify
        <code>gutter = 180</code>
        and <code>activeWidth = width - 360</code>
        across desktop, iPad, and landscape phone viewports.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>
      S2 — Independent pad states &amp; dual-thrust pointer support in
      <code>Input</code>
      (core, small)
    </h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Extend <code>packages/core/src/lib/entities/Input.ts</code> so
        <code>setPointer(id, zone)</code>
        accepts
        <code>"left" | "right" | "both" | null</code>
        and exposes per-pad active state (<code>leftTurn</code>,
        <code>rightTurn</code>,
        <code>forwardBurn</code>) alongside effective thruster outputs.
      </li>
      <li>
        Update <code>docs/specs/game/controls/spec.md</code> (and
        <code>docs/specs/game/spec.md</code>) with the three-zone touch and
        keyboard mapping.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Correctness:</strong>
        Unit tests verify single-pointer
        <code>left</code>, <code>right</code>, and <code>both</code> states,
        combined <code>both + left</code> highlighting, control inversion, and
        reset on blur/cancel.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>
      S3 — Disjoint 60° glass thrust pads in <code>FlightDeck</code> and Lab
      catalog (web &amp; core styles, medium; needs S1, S2)
    </h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Extract a shared <code>ThrustFlapPair.svelte</code> component
        implementing the accepted 02.6 design: disjoint borderless polygons
        inside the <code>180 CSS px</code> gutter (<code
          >84% W = 151 CSS px</code
        >,
        <code>54% H</code>
        corner-nested <code>F</code>), stepped resting blue glass (<code
          >22%</code
        >
        / <code>36%</code>), thin active yellow wash (<code>9%</code>
        / <code>16%</code>), <code>72 CSS px</code>
        border-aligned white technical markings, and end-to-end 30°-seam pointer
        capture.
      </li>
      <li>
        Wire <code>ThrustFlapPair</code> into <code>FlightDeck.svelte</code> and
        demonstrate the shared component in the Lab catalog.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Correctness &amp; fidelity:</strong>
        Browser tests verify independent pad highlighting for <code>L</code>,
        <code>R</code>,
        <code>F</code>, and <code>F + L</code>, end-to-end touch hit-testing
        across the full <code>180 CSS px</code> width, and visual fidelity
        matching the accepted 02.6 reference at <code>1280px+</code> in the Lab.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
ul {
  max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  margin-block: 0 var(--spacing-md);
  padding-left: var(--spacing-md);
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--spacing-xs);
}

.prior-iteration {
  margin-block: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-lg);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  background: var(--color-elev-1);
}

.prior-iteration summary {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md);
  font-family: var(--font-family-ui);
  font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  cursor: pointer;
}

.details-body {
  padding: var(--spacing-md);
  border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
}
</style>
